## Why This Is Asked
This problem combines two key challenges: optimizing over two sequences simultaneously and selecting exactly k non-overlapping intervals. It tests your ability to formulate dynamic programming solutions for constrained partitioning problems and handle solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- 2D prefix sums for O(1) range sum queries
- Non-overlapping interval DP with exact count constraint
- Path reconstruction via backpointers
- Trade-off between intervals via knapsack-like combining

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxIntervalValue(A, B, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const PA = [0], PB = [0];
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    PA[i] = PA[i-1] + A[i-1];
    PB[i] = PB[i-1] + B[i-1];
  }
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let r = 1; r <= n; r++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= r; l++) {
        const val = (PA[r] - PA[l-1]) * (PB[r] - PB[l-1]);
        const candidate = dp[l-1][t-1] + val;
        if (candidate > dp[r][t]) {
          dp[r][t] = candidate;
          prev[r][t] = { l, r: l-1 };
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow diagonal moves?
- How would you adapt for multiple starting points or 3D grids?

## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with partitioning; tests clear recurrence and reconstruction, common in interviews. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t] = min over p of dp[p][t-1] + max(A[p..i-1]);
- Track parent to reconstruct partition points; iterate i and t in increasing order.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(cand < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a deque-based technique when max is a sliding property? 
- How would you extend to allow hypothetical negative numbers or to reconstruct explicit subarrays?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a tree DP with multiple subtrees and an exact count constraint. Requires knapsack-style merging across children and careful reconstruction to report all k endpoints. Also checks edge-disjointness handling and correctness when k exceeds leaves.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with per-node knapsack merge
- Exact-path counting and edge-disjointness
- Result reconstruction: path endpoints
- Complexity reasoning: O(n k^2) time, O(n k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, k, val, children, dp) {
  let f = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
  f[0] = 0;
  for (const v of children[u]) {
    let g = Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
      if (f[i] <= -Infinity) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t + i <= k; t++) {
        g[i+t] = Math.max(g[i+t], f[i] + dp[v][t]);
      }
    }
    f = g;
  }
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[u][t] = f[t] + t * val[u];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the DP if paths could start at any node but must end at a leaf? 
- How would you adapt reconstruction to output the exact endpoints efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to design multi-dimensional DP by combining deletion constraints with pairwise scoring across adjacent characters. It evaluates state space design, transition logic, and reconstruction from a full solution path.

## Key Concepts
- **State design**: A 3D DP tracks position i (0 to n), deletions used d (0 to k), and the last kept character L (0–25, or 26 for none).
- **Transition logic**: The delete transition preserves state; the keep transition accumulates score only when a previous character exists, using W[prev][cur].
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking from the optimal endpoint to determine which characters were deleted.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(s, W, k) {
    const n = s.length, NONE = 26;
    const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () =>
        Array.from({length: k+1}, () =>
            Array(NONE+1).fill(-Infinity)));
    dp[0][0][NONE] = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
            for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++) {
                if (dp[i][d][L] === -Infinity) continue;
                // Delete
                if (d < k) dp[i+1][d+1][L] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d+1][L], dp[i][d][L]);
                // Keep
                const cur = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                const gain = L === NONE ? 0 : W[L][cur];
                dp[i+1][d][cur] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d][cur], dp[i][d][L] + gain);
            }
        }
    }
    // Find best among d ≤ k
    let best = 0, bestD = 0, bestL = NONE;
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++)
        for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++)
            if (dp[n][d][L] > best) { best = dp[n][d][L]; bestD = d; bestL = L; }
    return { score: best, deletions: bestD, lastKept: bestL };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Memory optimization**: Can you reduce space to O(27·k) using rolling arrays?
- **Negative weights**: How would you handle cases where W contains negative values—would your approach change?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP on strings, palindrome precomputation, and reconstruction. It also requires handling a non-trivial scoring function beyond simple lengths.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome table isPal[i][j] for substring s[i..j]
- dist[i][j] stores number of distinct characters in s[i..j]
- DP state dp[i][t]: best score for first i chars partitioned into t palindromes
- Transition: for all j < i with isPal[j][i-1], dp[i][t] = max(dp[i][t], dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1])
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to recover cut positions
- Time/space: O(n^2 k) time and O(n^2 k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalPartitionScore(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { isPal[i][i] = true; dist[i][i] = 1; }
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (len === 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
      const seen = new Set();
      for (let p = i; p <= j; p++) seen.add(s[p]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (isPal[j][i-1] && dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
          const cand = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push([j, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return { score: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory with rolling DP when n is large?
- How does the approach adapt if the score uses a different function, e.g., length of palindrome minus distinct count?
- What are the practical limits for n and k for this to run within time constraints?

## Why This Problem Matters
This problem assesses sophisticated dynamic programming techniques by combining palindrome preprocessing, optimal substructure for non-overlapping segment selection, and explicit solution reconstruction. It integrates preprocessing with suffix-based DP while requiring careful index management.

## Key Concepts
- Palindrome table (isPal) DP preprocessing for O(1) palindrome queries
- 2D DP with optimal substructure and complete solution reconstruction
- Complexity analysis: Time O(n²k), Space O(n² + nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalBlocks(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  // Precompute palindrome table
  for (let len = 1; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len <= n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      isPal[l][r] = (s[l] === s[r] && (len <= 2 || isPal[l+1][r-1]));
    }
  }

  // Initialize DP tables
  const dp = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  const trace = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Fill DP table
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i + 1][t];
      trace[i][t] = {type: 'skip'};

      for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
        if (isPal[i][j]) {
          const val = (j - i + 1) + dp[j + 1][t - 1];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            trace[i][t] = {type: 'take', l: i, r: j};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = 0, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i < n) {
    const choice = trace[i][t];
    if (choice.type === 'take') {
      blocks.push([choice.l, choice.r]);
      i = choice.r + 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }

  return {maxLength: dp[0][k], blocks};
}
```
